{"annotationDigest":"sha256:f545b257b10e3c179887a1fb7c6d96fa62b582f2bb0b22d192eac3e127ee7c85","annotationId":"rann-c7306aa0fbcb8481ef462de7ae1720aa","author":{"agent":"claude-code","kind":"ai","model":"claude-opus-5.5"},"createdAt":"2026-10-08T05:50:01Z","disclaimer":"AI-generated review opinion derived from the evidence package. It is not a verdict, does not change readiness, and must be confirmed by a human reviewer.","disclosureCheck":{"blocking":0,"contradictions":[],"disclosures":4,"nextDecision":"human_judgement","readiness":"ready"},"findings":[{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP","via":"bin/omarchy-system-lock:lock_ipc"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"Base line 10 `omarchy-shell lock lock >/dev/null` is replaced by `lock_reply=$(lock_ipc 1s lock lock)` (line 39). The new `lock_ipc` (line 13) bounds every call with OMARCHY_SHELL_IPC_TIMEOUT and drops the wrapper's stderr. After the unchanged layout reset, 1Password lock and ttfx teardown, a `missing-pam` reply fails at once (line 72). Otherwise the script polls `lock_state` (line 21) until a 10 s deadline (line 77). This matches the PR text: \"The command now polls `lock status` until `.secure` is true.\"","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":13},{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":39},{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":77},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"info","title":"The fix: omarchy-system-lock now succeeds only once lock status reports the session secure (SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP)","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP is in review.changes (code_changed, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SW-REQ-261006-861H","via":"bin/omarchy-system-lock:report_unsecured"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"861H: \"omarchy-system-lock shall exit 0 only after the shell's lock status reports the session secure. When the shell refuses the lock, or the session is not secure within 10 s, the script shall exit 1. It shall then also send the critical 'Screen did not lock' notification.\" It satisfies T0XP and traces to lock_state and report_unsecured (Implements lines, plus one on the deadline loop at line 76). REVIEW-261006-RY53 reads the script's two exits against the FRETish biconditionals. Its rationale says the notification \"is shown only while the shell, which owns the notification server, is running\". It requires sending, not delivery. That gap is a question below.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":29},{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":81},{"id":"proof/reviews/REVIEW-261006-RY53.yaml","kind":"file","line":1},{"id":"specs/software/requirements/SW-REQ-261006-861H.req.yaml","kind":"file","line":8},{"id":"SW-REQ-261006-861H","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"info","title":"SW-REQ-261006-861H (added, proof work) states the failure contract under T0XP","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-261006-861H","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-261006-861H is in review.changes (added, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SYS-REQ-260927-WC89","via":"bin/omarchy-system-lock:report_unsecured"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"Base text: \"The callee engages the session lock and exits 0.\" Evaluated text: \"The callee exits 0 once the session lock is secure. When the lock does not hold, it exits 1 and shows a notification (SW-REQ-261006-861H).\" The mirror's proof work also removed WC89's error_handling deferral to KI-260930-2FBE and set verification to passing. SW-REQ-260922-N3RM, the menu side that satisfies WC89, was re-reviewed after the text change; its own code is unchanged. Opinion: \"shows a notification\" holds only while the shell is up (see the question below); \"sends\", as in 861H, would be exact.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":29},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260927-WC89","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-N3RM","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"STK-REQ-260912-XJ5D","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"info","title":"SYS-REQ-260927-WC89 is modified: its text now covers the failure path","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SYS-REQ-260927-WC89","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SYS-REQ-260927-WC89 is in review.changes (modified, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SW-REQ-260912-MXQG","via":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh:run_variant"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"`pkill -x ttfx`, the bounded `timeout 1s pidwait -x ttfx` and the screensaver window kill (lines 58-63) are untouched. They now run before the secure check, so they also run when the lock fails. The PR says so (\"screensaver teardown still run before either exit path\"). The function-level match is through the test helpers run_variant and variant_bin, which re-host the baseline MXQG witnesses on the PR's shell mock.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":58},{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":185},{"id":"SW-REQ-260912-MXQG","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"info","title":"SW-REQ-260912-MXQG: the ttfx teardown is unchanged and still runs before either exit","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260912-MXQG","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-260912-MXQG is in review.changes (code_changed, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"detail":"The `locking)` arm (line 82) waits without asking again while the shell reports a pending request. That is the state #10299 reports: `lockRequested` stays latched and `lock()` answers `ok` without locking (Service.qml:923-929). The PR's own mock prints `requested: true` only with `secure: true`, so the PR's test never reaches this arm. The mirror's test adds a REQUESTED switch to the mock (line 53) and the case 'system lock fails when an accepted lock never secures' (line 167). My runs: changing line 81 to `secure|locking) exit 0 ;;` now fails that case. On the upstream head alone, the PR's test passes 9/9 with or without the mutation; adding the same case gives 10/10 on the head and fails on quattro 035ce29f (exit 0). Upstream would need the test to keep the guard.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":82},{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":167},{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":53},{"id":"shell/plugins/lock/Service.qml","kind":"file","line":923},{"id":"SW-REQ-261006-861H","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"info","title":"The #10299 state (`requested: true`, never `secure`) is now pinned, by our proof-layer test only","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-system-lock:report_unsecured","bin/omarchy-notification-send"],"scenario":"shell process not running (or crashed) when the user presses Super+Ctrl+L or closes the lid: the script exits 1 after ~10 s and calls omarchy-notification-send while no process owns org.freedesktop.Notifications; is any on-screen warning shown?"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"report_unsecured sends the warning with omarchy-notification-send (line 32), which calls org.freedesktop.Notifications over busctl. The Omarchy shell is that server (docs/notifications.md:3: \"The shell is the notification daemon\"). omarchy-crash-watch says a toast sent while the shell is down \"is lost\" (line 28), the repo ships no D-Bus activation for it, and `|| true` hides the failure. 861H requires sending the notification, and its rationale says it is shown only while the shell runs. All four product callers drop the exit code. The lid-close helper runs `omarchy-system-lock >/dev/null 2>&1 || true`. The idle service only logs it. The menu row and Super+Ctrl+L run it detached. The menu and the idle timer are part of the shell, so they cannot fire then anyway. For the 'shell not running' failure, outside a terminal, the PR changes only the exit code. This comes from reading; I did not run a live session.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":32},{"id":"docs/notifications.md","kind":"file","line":3},{"id":"bin/omarchy-crash-watch","kind":"file","line":28},{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lid-close","kind":"file","line":17},{"id":"shell/plugins/services/idle/Service.qml","kind":"file","line":311},{"id":"SW-REQ-261006-861H","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260927-WC89","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: when the shell is down, is the exit code alone an acceptable failure signal?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Shell down: is the failure visible anywhere?\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-system-lock","SW-REQ-260912-MXQG"],"scenario":"a lock the shell refuses (missing-pam) or never secures while ttfx is running: the head closes the screensaver and exits 1, leaving the unlocked desktop visible; should the screensaver stay up instead?"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"The head closes ttfx and the screensaver window before it knows whether the lock held, so a failed lock leaves the desktop visible behind the notification. This is intended: the PR says the teardown \"still run[s] before either exit path\", and the BASELINE texts of MXQG and T0XP require the teardown whenever the user locks. The alternative #13756 keeps the screensaver up on a refusal. My runs: moving the teardown into the `secure)` arm keeps exit codes and notifications the same. It flips 2 assertions in the PR's own test on the upstream head: the block 'a lock the shell refuses still runs the rest of the lock sequence' and 'still closes the screensaver when the lock fails'. On the mirror a third flips: the baseline MXQG witness 'still stops the screensaver when the lock IPC fails'. Taking that change would also need MXQG/T0XP amended. That is a maintainer's choice.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":58},{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":132},{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":160},{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":282},{"id":"SW-REQ-260912-MXQG","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: should a lock that did not hold still close the screensaver?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Screensaver state after a refused lock\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-system-lock:lock_state","bin/omarchy-system-lock"],"scenario":"session secured and unlocked by fingerprint within the ~1 s before the first lock-status poll: does the script send a second lock lock and re-lock the session?"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"If the session is secured and then unlocked (for example by fingerprint) before the first `lock status`, the poll reads `requested: false, secure: false`. The `*)` arm (line 83) then sends another `lock lock`, so the user must unlock twice. The script cannot tell this from a request the shell dropped. The window is short: the first poll comes after the ttfx teardown (up to 1 s), and the shell needs time to secure. It fails safe (locked again). omarchybot raised it on the PR. No test pins the chosen behaviour.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":83},{"id":"shell/plugins/lock/Service.qml","kind":"file","line":937},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261006-861H","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: an unlock before the first poll gets a second lock","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Unlock before the first poll\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-system-lid-close","bin/omarchy-system-lock"],"scenario":"lid closed with the shell refusing or never securing: omarchy-hyprland-monitor-clamshell starts only after omarchy-system-lock gives up (~10 s)"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Base returned right after the teardown. The head returns after `secure` (normally fast) or after the 10 s deadline plus one in-flight IPC call. bin/omarchy-system-lid-close runs the lock synchronously and only then `omarchy-hyprland-monitor-clamshell` (line 17-19), so a failed lock delays clamshell reconciliation. The PR states this. The idle service skips a new lock while lockProcess is still running (Service.qml:60). Bounded, but untested at the caller level.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lid-close","kind":"file","line":17},{"id":"shell/plugins/services/idle/Service.qml","kind":"file","line":60},{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":77},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: callers now wait up to ~10 s when the lock fails; is that acceptable for the lid-close helper and the idle service?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Lid-close timing with a failing lock\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-system-lock:lock_ipc"],"scenario":"run omarchy-system-lock in a terminal with OMARCHY_PATH unset or the shell stopped: base prints the wrapper's reason; head prints only 'the shell did not secure the session within 10s'"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Removed-behaviour inventory of base line 10 `omarchy-shell lock lock >/dev/null`. It made (a) one request; (b) passed the wrapper's stderr through (\"omarchy-shell is not running\", \"OMARCHY_PATH is not set\", \"not responding\"); (c) used the wrapper's default IPC timeout; (d) ignored the reply and status, and the script exited 0. The PR intends (a), (c) and (d): repeated requests, 1 s/0.5 s bounds, exit 1 (now codified by 861H). (b) is not mentioned. `lock_ipc` sends stderr to /dev/null (line 17), and the script prints only its own two reasons. No test pins (b). The KI's mitigation text still says the terminal \"shows the callee stderr directly\". The package's removed_behavior source recorded no inventory for this shell change.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":17},{"id":"proof/known-issues/KI-260930-2FBE.yaml","kind":"file","line":16},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Removed behaviour: the wrapper's own stderr no longer reaches the caller (unverified)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Terminal diagnostics when the wrapper fails\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"detail":"All four compat sources ran with 0 findings, but none of them reaches bin/omarchy-system-lock. (1) The differential harness only runs shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml and MenuModel.js, so its 213 inputs never run the lock script. (2) test_diff reports \"the change modifies or deletes no existing test file\", yet system-lock-test.sh is changed. The engine's test_diff parses Go, Python and JS tests only, so the shell test is skipped. One existing expectation flips there: the lock-IPC-failure block used to need the script to complete (exit 0 under `set -e`). Now it needs exit 1 and the notification. That is the PR's purpose (861H), so I read it as intended, but no source records it. (3) mcdc_diff counts 92 decisions at base and 93 at head, while coverage of the script is unmeasured (coverage_unmeasured). This is my reading of the engine's limits; it does not change the package's compatibility state.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":282},{"id":"test/bdiff/harness.mjs","kind":"file","line":1},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261006-861H","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Opinion: the package's 'compatible' does not cover this change","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"On copies of the evaluated tree (stub harness, bash 5): system-lock-test.sh passes 17/17. With the base script it fails at 'system lock fails when no lock screen is configured'. Removing the missing-pam check fails the same assertion. Removing the re-request fails 're-requests a lock the shell dropped'. Disabling the notification fails 'warns on screen when it could not lock'. Treating `locking` as success fails 'fails when an accepted lock never secures'. The KI-260930-2FBE reproducer exits 0 (defect present) on the base script and 1 on the head; its healthy control passes on both. This is evidence about test strength, not engine-verified.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":117},{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":154},{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":167},{"id":"pocs/wc89-silent-lock-exit0.sh","kind":"file","line":50},{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"test"}],"severity":"info","title":"My own runs: the mirror's tests catch each single-part removal, and the KI reproducer flips","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"testResults: ingested, current, 68 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped; 2 tagged tests, both joined. MXQG, 861H, T0XP and WC89 have error_handling and nominal observed_pass. WC89 nominal comes through STK-REQ-260912-XJ5D from sleep-lock-test.sh. Boundary on T0XP and WC89 is not_linked: no test is tagged with that class, so it is not tested evidence. The 10 s deadline is a boundary that no tagged test pins.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260912-MXQG","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261006-861H","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260912-T0XP","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260927-WC89","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"test"}],"severity":"info","title":"Obligation cells: 8 observed_pass, 2 not_linked (boundary on T0XP and WC89)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"claim":{"params":{"function":"make_shell","path":"pocs/wc89-silent-lock-exit0.sh","position":"intended"},"type":"unmapped_code"},"detail":"Changed on the mirror by 74ec7c2c, so that it models the lock IPC: the control answers `ok` and then reports `secure: true`. It is the evidence manifest of KI-260930-2FBE. Its make_shell helper is an untraced unit, and the