licable, all unchanged from the baseline. No failing test, in scope or out of scope.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"test"}],"severity":"info","title":"Nothing failed in this run","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}}],"packageDigest":"sha256:afadcf44658115da2876b427cb0f74a4270eb5b40471ca8a8e99c7bacc6c4112","packageIdentity":"epkg-52085becb88e20928b31217d37f71421bd75b41c1e1a9618b88206f665124cf5","riskAssessment":{"level":"medium","rationale":"A small, security-relevant fix. Its contract is now stated (861H), and the mirror's tests catch each single-part removal. The guard for the #10299 state, however, is in our proof-layer test, not in the PR. Coverage of the changed script was not measured, and the compatibility evidence does not exercise it. When the shell itself is down, the on-screen warning probably cannot be delivered. Other trade-offs are intended and bounded: screensaver teardown on failure, a re-lock after a very fast unlock, and a ~10 s wait on failure. This assessment is advisory only."},"schemaVersion":"reqproof.review-annotation.v1","suggestedTests":[{"forClaim":5,"rationale":"In a session with the shell stopped, run omarchy-system-lock and check busctl's result for the Notify call (or that no org.freedesktop.Notifications owner exists). If it fails, decide whether the warning needs a route that does not depend on the shell.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":32},{"id":"docs/notifications.md","kind":"file","line":3}],"title":"Shell down: is the failure visible anywhere?"},{"forClaim":6,"rationale":"Pin the chosen behaviour either way. Today the PR's tests assert the teardown on failure. If the maintainers prefer #13756's behaviour, the same assertions invert, and MXQG/T0XP need amending.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":132},{"id":"test/shell.d/system-lock-test.sh","kind":"file","line":160}],"title":"Screensaver state after a refused lock"},{"forClaim":7,"rationale":"Mock: secure on poll 1, then `requested: false` (unlocked). Assert how many `lock lock` calls follow, and pin the decision with a comment at the `*)` arm.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":83}],"title":"Unlock before the first poll"},{"forClaim":8,"rationale":"In lid-close-test.sh, make the omarchy-system-lock mock take the full deadline. Assert that clamshell reconciliation still runs, and record how long it waits.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lid-close","kind":"file","line":17}],"title":"Lid-close timing with a failing lock"},{"forClaim":9,"rationale":"Run with OMARCHY_PATH unset and assert which reason reaches stderr, so dropping the wrapper's message is a decision, not an accident.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-system-lock","kind":"file","line":17}],"title":"Terminal diagnostics when the wrapper fails"}],"summary":"**What the PR does.** Upstream #9429 (VykosMolt) makes `omarchy-system-lock` fail when the session does not become secure. At base it sends `lock lock`, discards the reply and always exits 0. Now every IPC call is bounded (`lock_ipc`); a `missing-pam` reply fails at once; otherwise it polls `lock status` for up to 10 s: `secure` exits 0, `requested` waits, anything else asks again. On failure `report_unsecured` writes stderr, sends a critical \"Screen did not lock\" notification and exits 1. Layout reset, 1Password and screensaver teardown still run first. On the mirror this is f50c01aa (upstream's product diff plus 4 comment lines). Later commits are our proof work: the KI-260930-2FBE reproducer, KI fixed, new SW-REQ-261006-861H for the failure contract, WC89 text, a #10299 test, witnesses, trace reviews.\n\n**Is the fix verified?** Coverage of bin/omarchy-system-lock was not measured (coverage_unmeasured), so \"0 uncovered lines\" says nothing about it. 68 results: 68 passed. Cells of MXQG, 861H, T0XP and WC89 are observed_pass, except boundary (not_linked) on T0XP and WC89. My runs: system-lock-test.sh 17/17 at 6b50b5e5; it fails on the base script and when I remove the missing-pam check, the re-request or the notification, or treat `requested` as secure. That last case is caught only by the #10299 test, which is our proof-layer addition, not the PR's own test.\n\n**Compatibility.** The package states compatible (4 sources, 0 findings, 213 inputs). None of it reaches this change: the differential runs menu code only, test_diff does not parse shell tests, and the script's decisions are not measured. Readiness is ready; the next decision is human_judgement (acceptance) by a maintainer. Nothing here is an approval.\n\n**Questions for a human.** (1) With the shell down, the warning has no server: is exit code alone enough? (2) Should a failed lock still close the screensaver? (3) An unlock before the first poll gets a second lock. (4) Callers wait up to ~10 s on failure. (5) The wrapper's own stderr is now hidden.\n\n**Traceability.** All four requirements match at function level. 861H (added) states the contract; WC89's text now says the callee exits 1 and notifies when the lock does not hold. Change effects were not analyzed (effects_unavailable).\n\n**Not caused by this PR.** No check failed; 13 were skipped by configuration. pocs/wc89-silent-lock-exit0.sh is our reproducer, out of scope."}
